desc(
	mode: "source"
	language: "general"
	title: "Cookie Serialization"
	title_zh: "Cookie Serialization"
	type: vuln
	level: high
	rule_id: "source-insecure-api-cookie-serialization"
	risk: "deserialization"
	desc: <<<DESC
Migrated from rule `cookie-serialization` (ruby/lang/security/cookie-serialization.yaml).
Original message: Checks if code allows cookies to be deserialized using Marshal. If the attacker can craft a valid cookie, this could lead to remote code execution. The hybrid check is just to warn users to migrate to :json for best practice.
CWE: CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')
References:
- https://github.com/presidentbeef/brakeman/blob/main/lib/brakeman/checks/check_cookie_serialization.rb
- https://robertheaton.com/2013/07/22/how-to-hack-a-rails-app-using-its-secret-token/
Note: source-mode regex approximation of a safe-literal pattern.
DESC
	solution: <<<SOLUTION
Review this occurrence and replace it with a safer alternative.
SOLUTION
	alert_min: 1
	"file://pos.rb": <<<POS
Rails.application.config.action_dispatch.cookies_serializer = :marshal
POS
	"safefile://pos.rb": <<<NEG
Rails.application.config.action_dispatch.cookies_serializer = :json
NEG
)

${*.rb}.pattern_regex(/(?:Rails\.application\.config\.action_dispatch\.cookies_serializer\s+=\s+:marshal)|(?:Rails\.application\.config\.action_dispatch\.cookies_serializer\s+=\s+:hybrid)/) as $hit
alert $hit for {
	level: "high",
	title: "Cookie Serialization",
	title_zh: "Cookie Serialization",
	message: "Cookie Serialization detected.",
}
